    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Proclaimed a republic by the conservative caudillo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> in 1847, it is the most populous country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>.</li><li>Liberal dictators built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> economy on forced Maya labour and gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a> great privileges.</li><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1954-guatemalan-coup-detat">CIA-backed coup</a> in 1954 ended a decade of reform; civil war in 1960–1996 killed about 200,000 people, most of them Maya.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A young Argentine doctor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Ernesto Guevara</a>, was living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1954-guatemalan-coup-detat">CIA-backed coup</a> toppled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Árbenz</a> in 1954, an experience that radicalised the future ‘Che’.</p></aside>
    </section>

