    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide.jpg" alt="Agustín de Iturbide" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agustin_I_of_Mexico.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Primitivo Miranda, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> inherited the core of the Kingdom, or Captaincy General, of Guatemala, the Spanish jurisdiction that governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de los Caballeros (today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigua-guatemala">Antigua Guatemala</a>) and, after the earthquakes of 1773, from the new capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. After independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821 and a brief annexation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">Mexican Empire</a>, Guatemala became the largest and most populous state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>, whose government sat in Guatemala City until 1834. Liberal reforms under the state governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-galvez">Mariano Gálvez</a> provoked a peasant revolt in 1837, inflamed by a cholera epidemic and by attacks on the church, which brought the young mestizo leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> to prominence. Carrera’s forces took Guatemala City in 1838 and defeated the federal president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> there in March 1840, ending the federation in practice. Guatemala then functioned as an independent state, and on 21 March 1847 Carrera formally proclaimed the Republic of Guatemala. Briefly driven into exile in 1848, he returned in 1849, defeated a Honduran and Salvadoran invasion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-arada">Battle of La Arada</a> in 1851 and was named president for life in 1854. He ruled until his death in 1865 in alliance with the Catholic Church, the merchant elite of the capital and the indigenous communities whose lands he largely respected.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amatitlan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amatitlan.jpg" alt="Amatitlán" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amatitlan">Amatitlán</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amatitl%C3%A1n_-_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Montaje realizado por Merari Rene 0901., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> has the largest population in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, about 18 million people, and the largest indigenous share: speakers of K’iche’, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qeqchi-people">Q’eqchi</a>’, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaqchikel-people">Kaqchikel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mam-people">Mam</a> and some twenty other Mayan languages make up over 40 per cent of the population in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> figures, alongside a ladino majority, Garífuna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinca-people">Xinca</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Carrera</a>, exports of cochineal dye from the valleys around Antigua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amatitlan">Amatitlán</a> sustained the economy until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">synthetic dyes</a> ruined the trade in the 1850s and 1860s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a> replaced it: after 1871 liberal governments sold church and communal lands and, through forced labour drafts known as <em>mandamientos</em> and debt peonage, made highland Maya communities supply seasonal workers to coffee estates on the Pacific piedmont and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alta-verapaz">Alta Verapaz</a>, where German planters became prominent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-ubico">Jorge Ubico</a> replaced debt peonage in 1934 with vagrancy laws that obliged poorer peasants to work on estates for a set number of days each year. From the early twentieth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>, with its railway affiliate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-railways-of-central-america">International Railways of Central America</a> and banana plantations near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and Pacific coasts, became the country’s largest landowner and employer. Land remained extremely concentrated. Coffee, bananas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, cardamom and textiles are still major exports, and remittances from Guatemalans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> now equal nearly a fifth of national income.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa.jpg" alt="Battle of Chalchuapa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa">Battle of Chalchuapa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muertebarrios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Revista Guatemala, 1887, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> was the strongest military power of nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Carrera</a>’s armies intervened repeatedly in El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> and Honduras, defeating the liberal coalition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-arada">La Arada</a> in 1851 and taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> in 1863, and Guatemalan contingents joined the Central American war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> in 1856–1857. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a> built a modern army with a military academy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/escuela-politecnica-guatemala">Escuela Politécnica</a>, founded in 1873, and in 1885 tried to reunite Central America by force, only to be killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chalchuapa">Battle of Chalchuapa</a> in El Salvador. The border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was settled by treaty in 1882, with Guatemala renouncing its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xoconusco">Soconusco</a>, while the claim to British Honduras, today Belize, remained a long-standing grievance. In the twentieth century the army became the arbiter of politics. In June 1954 a small force under Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-castillo-armas">Carlos Castillo Armas</a>, organised and supplied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-intelligence-agency">CIA</a>, invaded from Honduras, and the army’s refusal to fight for President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a> decided the outcome. A failed officers’ revolt on 13 November 1960 gave rise to the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups, and the internal armed conflict lasted until 1996. Its worst phase came in 1981–1983, when the army’s counter-insurgency campaigns in the western highlands destroyed hundreds of Maya villages and forced men into civil defence patrols. The war killed or caused the disappearance of about 200,000 people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera.jpg" alt="Rafael Carrera" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carrerayturcios_2014-06-22_09-46_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Carrera</a>’s conservative regime restored the church’s privileges, signed a concordat with the Holy See in 1852 and governed through the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, the Consulado de Comercio, and local officials, with Carrera himself president for life from 1854. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1871">Liberal Revolution of 1871</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-garcia-granados">Miguel García Granados</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a>, overthrew Carrera’s successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-cerna-y-cerna">Vicente Cerna</a>. Barrios, president from 1873 to 1885, expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, confiscated church property, established secular education and civil marriage, and gave the liberal state its constitution of 1879. His successors continued a pattern of long liberal dictatorships: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-estrada-cabrera">Manuel Estrada Cabrera</a> ruled from 1898 until the assembly declared him insane in 1920, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-ubico">Jorge Ubico</a> from 1931 to 1944, both governing through the army, police informers and favours to foreign companies. The October Revolution of 1944 opened ten years of democracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-arevalo">Juan José Arévalo</a>, president from 1945 to 1951, introduced a new constitution, social security, a labour code and autonomy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-san-carlos-of-guatemala">University of San Carlos</a>, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a> enacted the agrarian reform of Decree 900 in 1952, which expropriated uncultivated land on large estates, including much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit</a>’s reserves. After the coup of 1954 the army dominated government, through generals elected in managed elections or seizing power, until the constitution of 1985 restored civilian rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinicio-cerezo">Vinicio Cerezo</a>, the first civilian president since 1970.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guatemala-city.jpg" alt="Guatemala City" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_de_Guatemala,_CA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rene Hernandez, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> is a reference point in debates on intervention and indigenous rights. The coup of 1954 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Árbenz</a>, organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-intelligence-agency">CIA</a> under the code name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1954-guatemalan-coup-detat">PBSuccess</a>, was one of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> interventions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>; it radicalised a generation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American revolutionaries, among them the young Ernesto ‘Che’ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Guevara</a>, who was living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> at the time, and became a standard case in the study of American foreign policy. The civil war produced another landmark: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commission-for-historical-clarification">Commission for Historical Clarification</a>, set up under the peace accords, reported in 1999 that state forces were responsible for over 90 per cent of the documented violations and that agents of the state had committed acts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> against Maya groups in 1981–1983. In 2013 the former dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/efrain-rios-montt">Efraín Ríos Montt</a> was convicted of genocide by a Guatemalan court, the first such verdict against a former head of state in a national court, although it was annulled days later. The K’iche’ activist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigoberta-menchu">Rigoberta Menchú</a> received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1992, and the novelist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-angel-asturias">Miguel Ángel Asturias</a>, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-senor-presidente">El Señor Presidente</a></em> portrays a dictatorship modelled on that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-estrada-cabrera">Estrada Cabrera</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1967. The UN-backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-commission-against-impunity-in-guatemala">International Commission against Impunity in Guatemala</a> (CICIG), active from 2007 to 2019, became a model for international anti-corruption efforts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jorge-serrano-elias"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jorge-serrano-elias.jpg" alt="Jorge Serrano Elías" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jorge-serrano-elias">Jorge Serrano Elías</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foto_oficial_de_Jorge_Antonio_Serrano_El%C3%ADas_(cropped_2).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gobierno de Guatemala, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> still exists, and its present form dates from the democratic transition and the end of the war. The constitution of 1985 created a Constitutional Court and a human rights ombudsman, and in 1993 these institutions defeated President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-serrano-elias">Jorge Serrano</a>’s attempt to dissolve Congress and the courts. Negotiations between the government and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemalan-national-revolutionary-unity">Guatemalan National Revolutionary Unity</a> (URNG), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> coalition formed in 1982, produced a series of accords culminating in the Agreement on a Firm and Lasting Peace of 29 December 1996, signed under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-arzu">Álvaro Arzú</a>. The accords promised recognition of indigenous identity and rights, but a referendum on the constitutional reforms needed to implement them was defeated in 1999. Two days after the Catholic Church published its own report on wartime atrocities in April 1998, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-gerardi">Juan Gerardi</a> was murdered. Organised crime, drug trafficking and corruption have since weighed on the state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-commission-against-impunity-in-guatemala">CICIG</a> investigations led to the resignation and arrest of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-perez-molina">Otto Pérez Molina</a> in 2015, but President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-morales">Jimmy Morales</a> expelled the commission, and prosecutors later pursued many of those who had fought corruption. In 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-arevalo">Bernardo Arévalo</a>, son of the reformist president of 1945–1951, won the presidency on an anti-corruption platform and, despite attempts by the public prosecutor’s office to block him, took office in January 2024. Guatemala remains a presidential republic with deep inequality and heavy emigration.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, the most populous state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, shows in concentrated form the region’s history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> dictatorship, foreign corporate power and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> intervention, and its civil war, with acts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> against the Maya, made it a landmark case for truth commissions and transitional justice.</em></p>

